00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:03.110 align:start position:0%
like many businesses across the valley  the las vegas farm  located on grand teton and tanaya has  been hit financially by the pandemic  eight news now reporter kate houston  spoke to the owner who says  these setbacks aren't stopping them from  moving forward with fall activities  the las vegas farm in the northwest is a  sanctuary for animals that have been  abused or neglected  every animal here comes to us in trouble  every animal  comes to us in a horrific state  sharon lynson bart owns the farm and has  dedicated her life to rehabilitating  animals  but it comes at a cost feed alone is  more than 7  thousand dollars a month due to the  pandemic the farm lost its steady stream  of field trips and weddings  without those sources of income it's  really difficult for us to be able to  feed and care for these animals that are  in trouble  lindsen bart is now looking towards fall  hoping it can give them the boost  they need during the fall season many  families come to the farm to feed  animals  like hazel here and pick some pumpkins  and despite the pandemic  the farm will be moving forward with  some family friendly activities  in a safe fashion lyndsen bar plans to  sell pumpkins but spread the area out to  keep families distanced she tells me the  farm has hosted fall festivals for over  40 years  she wants to continue that tradition  with the families that come in here  with this openness that we've got  everybody can stay far enough away from  each other  to be able to have a real fun day in the  meantime lynson bart is keeping the farm  open every weekend so the community can  browse their farmers market  and interact with the animals and that  will keep helping us until we can get  back into  our field trips and our weddings and the  other things that bring in  enough money so we can continue here  kate houston 8 news now  if you're interested in donating feed or  supplies you can head to the las vegas  farm dot com  they're also now looking for volunteers  for the upcoming fall season
 

